import med, lohi as L, dep_common as DC
NEW=("cann","diss","inh","stim","cath")
E=lambda o1,o2,p1,p2,c1,c2:{"onset":[o1,o2],"peak":[p1,p2],"comedown":[c1,c2]}
med.GENERIC.update({"cann":"THC activates CB1 cannabinoid receptors in the brain, which normally respond to the body's own endocannabinoids; it is stored in fat and processed by the liver.","diss":"It blocks NMDA glutamate receptors (or, for propofol and etomidate, strengthens GABA-A receptors), disconnecting sensory input from awareness.","inh":"Inhaled vapors pass through the lungs into the blood and reach the brain within seconds.","stim":"It raises dopamine and noradrenaline signaling in the brain, either by blocking their reuptake or by releasing them.","cath":"Synthetic cathinones release or block the reuptake of dopamine, noradrenaline and serotonin, like amphetamines or cocaine."})
med.EXTRA.update({
"cann":E("Medically, the onset depends on the route. {met} Inhaled THC reaches the brain within minutes; swallowed THC is absorbed slowly and turned by the liver into 11-hydroxy-THC, which is more potent and longer-lasting, which is why edibles feel stronger and start so much later.","Early body signs include a faster heartbeat, red eyes, dry mouth and, in some people, a drop in blood pressure on standing that can cause fainting.","Medically, the peak is when CB1 activation is greatest. Short-term memory, attention, coordination and time perception are most affected, and anxiety or paranoia can appear, especially with high-THC products or in people prone to psychosis.","Cannabis alone does not stop breathing, so fatal overdose is not expected, but emergency visits for panic, vomiting, fast heart rate, and psychosis are common, and children who eat edibles can become very sedated.","As levels fall, the effect fades gradually because THC slowly leaves fat stores. Many people feel tired, hungry or foggy.","Daily users can develop cannabis use disorder and, with heavy long-term use, cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome (repeated severe vomiting relieved by hot showers). Withdrawal causes irritability, poor sleep and low appetite for one to two weeks."),
"diss":E("Medically, the onset reflects how quickly the drug reaches the brain. {met} As NMDA receptors are blocked, signals from the senses stop being integrated normally, so the body feels distant and numb.","Early signs include unsteady walking, slurred speech, numbness, a faster heartbeat and raised blood pressure (for NMDA blockers), and nystagmus (jerky eye movements).","Medically, the peak is maximal dissociation: perception, body awareness and memory are profoundly altered, and at high levels people may be unable to move or respond (the 'K-hole' for ketamine). Pain is not felt, so injuries go unnoticed.","Dangers at the peak include falls, drowning, choking on vomit, and, with alcohol or other depressants, breathing problems. PCP and DXM can cause agitation, high body temperature and violence; propofol and etomidate stop breathing easily.","As levels fall, people 'come back' gradually, often confused, nauseated and unsteady, with disjointed memories.","Repeated ketamine use damages the bladder (ketamine cystitis) and can harm memory; regular dissociative use can cause lasting low mood and, in some, psychosis."),
"inh":E("Medically, the onset is extremely fast because the lungs deliver vapors straight to the brain. {met} Effects begin within seconds and resemble rapid alcohol intoxication.","Early signs are dizziness, lightheadedness, slurred speech, and a 'rush'; for poppers, flushing and a pounding head from widened blood vessels.","Medically, the peak is brief. Many solvents and propellants make the heart extra sensitive to adrenaline, so a sudden scare or exertion can trigger a fatal heart rhythm ('sudden sniffing death'), even the first time.","Oxygen can be displaced, and using a bag or covering the head can cause suffocation; vomiting while intoxicated can choke. Poppers can drop blood pressure dangerously, especially with erectile dysfunction medicines.","As the vapor is breathed out, effects fade within minutes, leaving headache, nausea and drowsiness.","Long-term solvent use damages the brain, nerves, liver, kidneys and bone marrow; regular nitrous oxide use depletes vitamin B12 and can cause spinal cord and nerve damage."),
"stim":E("Medically, the onset reflects how fast the drug reaches the brain. {met} Rising dopamine and noradrenaline increase alertness, heart rate and blood pressure.","Early signs include widened pupils, a faster pulse, dry mouth, jaw tension and reduced appetite.","Medically, the peak is when heart rate, blood pressure and body temperature are highest. This is when heart attack, stroke, dangerous heart rhythms and overheating are most likely, especially with exertion, heat, or other stimulants.","High levels can cause anxiety, paranoia, repetitive behaviors and psychosis. Stimulant overdose has no antidote like naloxone, but cooling, calming and emergency care save lives; many 'stimulant' deaths now also involve fentanyl.","As levels fall, the 'crash' brings fatigue, low mood, irritability and hunger, often with poor sleep.","After binges, the crash can last days, with depression and strong cravings. Repeated use can cause dependence, weight loss, dental problems and heart damage."),
"cath":E("Medically, the onset depends on route. {met} Cathinones are beta-keto relatives of amphetamine; many cross into the brain quickly, causing a strong rush of energy and euphoria.","Early signs are a racing heart, sweating, jaw clenching and enlarged pupils.","Medically, the peak brings the highest risk of overheating, dangerous heart rhythms, high blood pressure, agitation and psychosis ('excited delirium' in emergency reports). Strong compulsion to redose is typical.","Severe cases cause muscle breakdown (rhabdomyolysis), kidney failure, and death. Products sold as 'bath salts' or as 'MDMA' often contain unknown cathinones.","As levels fall, people crash hard, with anxiety, low mood, insomnia and cravings.","Binges can last days without sleep, leading to paranoia, hallucinations and long recovery times.")})
V="Why the same amount affects people differently: tolerance, body size, genetics, heart health, mental health history, sleep, heat, hydration, and other drugs (including antidepressants and alcohol) all change the effect. Street products vary hugely in strength and content."
med.VARY.update({k:V for k in NEW})
L.CLS.update({
"cann":"At lower doses cannabis brings relaxation, mild euphoria, giggliness, and heightened senses. At higher doses, time distortion, anxiety, paranoia, a racing heart, nausea, dizziness and, for some, hallucinations or panic appear. Very high doses, especially edibles, can cause hours of confusion, vomiting and frightening psychotic symptoms.",
"diss":"At lower doses dissociatives bring numbness, lightness, mild euphoria and dreamy detachment. At higher doses, the body feels far away, movement becomes difficult, and vision and sound distort. At still higher levels, people lose contact with surroundings entirely, cannot move or respond, and are at risk of injury, vomiting and choking, with breathing danger when combined with depressants.",
"inh":"At lower exposures inhalants cause brief dizziness, giggling and lightheadedness. At higher exposures, confusion, hallucinations, loss of coordination and unconsciousness occur, and the risk of suffocation and sudden heart rhythm death rises sharply.",
"stim":"At lower doses stimulants increase alertness, focus, energy and confidence and reduce appetite. At higher doses euphoria, talkativeness, restlessness, jaw clenching, sweating, a pounding heart and anxiety increase. At very high levels, paranoia, hallucinations, overheating, chest pain, seizures, heart attack and stroke can occur.",
"cath":"At lower doses cathinones bring energy, euphoria and sociability. At higher doses, agitation, a racing heart, sweating, paranoia and a strong urge to redose dominate. At very high levels, psychosis, overheating, seizures, muscle breakdown and heart failure can occur."})
TOL={"cann":"Tolerance to the high builds quickly with daily use and fades after a break of a few weeks; people returning after a break, or switching to concentrates or edibles, are often surprised by how strong the effect is.",
"diss":"Tolerance to dissociatives builds quickly, and ketamine tolerance leads people to use more, raising the risk of bladder and memory damage.",
"inh":"Tolerance can develop with regular use, but the danger of sudden death does not decrease.",
"stim":"Tolerance to the euphoria builds quickly, while the heart and blood-pressure effects remain. Regular users often chase the first high and use more often.",
"cath":"Tolerance and compulsive redosing develop quickly with cathinones, and binges are common."}
SAFE={"cann":("cannabis-safety.md","Cannabis safety: edibles, high-potency products, synthetic cannabinoids"),"diss":("dissociative-safety.md","Dissociative safety: K-holes, injuries, bladder damage, mixing"),"inh":("inhalant-safety.md","Inhalant safety: sudden sniffing death, poppers, nitrous oxide"),"stim":("stimulant-safety.md","Stimulant safety: overheating, heart risks, psychosis, fentanyl contamination"),"cath":("stimulant-safety.md","Stimulant safety: overheating, heart risks, psychosis, fentanyl contamination")}
OD={"cann":"## Signs that someone needs help\n\n- Panic, chest pain, fainting, or a very fast heartbeat.\n- Severe confusion, paranoia, or hallucinations.\n- Repeated vomiting.\n- A child who may have eaten an edible.\n- With synthetic cannabinoids: seizures, unresponsiveness, or violent agitation.\n\n## What to do\n\n- Stay calm, move to a quiet place, and reassure them.\n- Call 911 for chest pain, seizures, unresponsiveness, or a child. Poison Control: 1-800-222-1222.\n- Use the recovery position if they are drowsy and vomiting.\n",
"stim":"## Signs of a stimulant emergency\n\n- Chest pain, a racing or irregular heartbeat, or severe headache.\n- Very high body temperature, hot dry skin, or confusion.\n- Seizures, extreme agitation, or paranoia.\n- Sudden weakness or trouble speaking (possible stroke).\n- Drowsiness and slow breathing (possible fentanyl contamination).\n\n## What to do\n\n- Call 911. Good Samaritan laws in most US states protect callers.\n- Move them somewhere cool, loosen clothing, and use cool water on the skin.\n- Keep them calm; do not restrain them.\n- Give naloxone if they are unresponsive or breathing slowly, since stimulants are often contaminated with fentanyl.\n"}
OD["cath"]=OD["stim"]
OD["diss"]="## Signs that someone needs help\n\n- Unresponsive, or breathing slowly or noisily.\n- Vomiting while dazed.\n- Very high temperature, seizures, or extreme agitation (especially PCP or DXM).\n- Injury they cannot feel.\n\n## What to do\n\n- Call 911. Good Samaritan laws in most US states protect callers.\n- Put them in the recovery position and keep them away from water, stairs and roads.\n- Give naloxone if opioids may be involved.\n"
OD["inh"]="## Signs that someone needs help\n\n- Unconscious, not breathing normally, or no pulse.\n- Chest pain, irregular heartbeat, or collapse.\n- Seizures or severe confusion.\n\n## What to do\n\n- Call 911 and start CPR if they are not breathing.\n- Do not chase or startle someone who is intoxicated on inhalants; a sudden adrenaline surge can stop the heart.\n- Get them into fresh air and remove any bag or covering.\n"
RT={"oral":"Swallowed: slower onset and longer effects; harder to judge, which tempts people to take more.","inhaled-smoked":"Smoked or vaped: effects in seconds to minutes, shorter duration, and strong reinforcement; damages the lungs and airways.","intranasal":"Snorted: faster onset than swallowing; damages the nose and sinuses.","injected":"Injected: the fastest and most intense route, with the highest risks of overdose, infection, and vein damage. Medical injections are given with monitoring.","sublingual-buccal":"Under the tongue or in the cheek: faster than swallowing.","rectal":"Rectal: a misuse route with unpredictable absorption.","transdermal":"Through the skin, as with nicotine patches: slow and steady."}
RK={"oral":"Delayed effects tempt redosing; edibles and long-acting pills are a common cause of accidental overdose and emergency visits.","inhaled-smoked":"Lung and airway damage, burns, and very fast, compulsive use patterns.","intranasal":"Nasal damage, nosebleeds, sinus infections, and shared straws can spread hepatitis C.","injected":"Infection, abscesses, endocarditis, HIV and hepatitis C from shared equipment, and very fast overdose.","sublingual-buccal":"Faster, stronger effect.","rectal":"Unpredictable absorption and tissue irritation.","transdermal":"Heat or damaged patches can release more drug; children are at risk from used patches."}
